INSERT INTO public.mock_test_questions (mock_test_id, question_text, options, question_type, correct_answer, explanation, topic_id, module_id, question_order, pairs, matches)
VALUES
  ('mock_36_all_1775004784055', 'A company is deploying a critical web application using AWS CodeDeploy to Amazon EC2 instances within an Auto Scaling group. They need to minimize downtime during deployments and ensure that if a new deployment introduces errors, the application automatically reverts to the last known good state without manual intervention. Which CodeDeploy configuration achieves MINIMUM downtime and automated recovery?', '["Use an in-place deployment strategy with manual monitoring and rollback.","Implement a blue/green deployment strategy with automated rollback triggers based on Amazon CloudWatch alarms.","Use a rolling update deployment strategy without any health checks.","Deploy directly to production without a staging environment to speed up releases."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) describes a robust deployment strategy. A blue/green deployment strategy minimizes downtime by deploying the new version to a separate environment and then shifting traffic. Integrating automated rollback triggers with Amazon CloudWatch alarms ensures that if key metrics (e.g., error rates, latency) breach thresholds, CodeDeploy automatically reverts to the stable ''blue'' environment, addressing the anti-pattern ([ap-1.1-002]) of not implementing automated rollbacks. Option A (incorrect) uses an in-place deployment, which typically involves downtime, and relies on manual intervention for rollback, increasing recovery time. Option C (incorrect) a rolling update without health checks is risky as it can propagate errors across instances without detection. Option D (incorrect) is an anti-pattern ([ap-1.2-001]) as deploying directly to production without proper testing and validation in a staging environment significantly increases the risk of production incidents.', 36, 'm_36_1', 6, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_36_all_1775004784055', 'A DevOps team is designing a multi-stage AWS CodePipeline for a new application. Each stage (Source, Build, Test, Deploy) requires specific AWS service permissions. The security team mandates strict adherence to the principle of least privilege to limit the blast radius of any compromised credentials. Which IAM configuration ensures the MOST secure pipeline with least privilege?', '["Assign a single IAM role with administrator access to the entire AWS CodePipeline.","Create a single IAM role for the pipeline and attach a policy granting broad permissions to all necessary services.","Configure separate, granular IAM roles for each AWS CodePipeline action, granting only the necessary permissions.","Use AWS Secrets Manager to store all credentials for each stage and retrieve them at runtime with a single IAM role."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) adheres to the principle of least privilege by configuring separate, granular IAM roles for each AWS CodePipeline action. This ensures that each stage or action only has the permissions it absolutely needs, minimizing the blast radius if a role is compromised. Option A (incorrect) and Option B (incorrect) represent the anti-pattern ([ap-1.1-001]) of using a single pipeline role with broad or admin access. This creates a large blast radius if compromised, violating security best practices. Option D (incorrect) uses AWS Secrets Manager for credentials, which is good, but combining it with a single, broadly permissive IAM role for the pipeline still violates the principle of least privilege for the pipeline''s execution role itself.', 36, 'm_36_1', 7, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_36_all_1775004784055', 'A software company has multiple microservices, each with its own AWS CodeBuild project. During peak development times, developers frequently push changes, leading to long build queues and slow feedback. They want to optimize build times by reusing dependencies and intermediate build outputs. Which AWS CodeBuild feature should they implement for the MOST efficient build performance?', '["Increase the compute capacity of each AWS CodeBuild project to always use larger instances.","Configure AWS CodeBuild to use batch builds for all microservices.","Disable caching in AWS CodeBuild to ensure fresh builds every time.","Enable caching for AWS CodeBuild projects, storing build artifacts in an Amazon S3 bucket."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{3}'::integer[], 'Option D (correct) leverages AWS CodeBuild''s caching feature, which allows storing dependencies and intermediate build outputs in an Amazon S3 bucket or locally within the build environment. This significantly reduces build times for subsequent builds by reusing cached data, leading to more efficient performance. Option A (incorrect) might reduce build time for individual builds but does not address the underlying issue of redundant dependency downloads and can be more costly. Option B (incorrect) batch builds are for running multiple builds concurrently or in sequence from a single start, which might help with throughput but not necessarily individual build duration due to dependency downloads. Option C (incorrect) disabling caching would have the opposite effect, increasing build times by forcing a full download and rebuild of dependencies every time.', 36, 'm_36_1', 8, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_36_all_1775004784055', 'A team is using AWS CodeBuild to compile a large Java application with many external dependencies. Each build downloads these dependencies, significantly increasing build times. They want to reduce build duration by caching these dependencies. Which AWS CodeBuild configuration should they use for the FASTEST build times for dependency-heavy projects?', '["Increase the build environment''s compute type to a larger instance size.","Configure a cache for the AWS CodeBuild project, pointing to an Amazon S3 bucket or local cache.","Use AWS CodeArtifact to store all dependencies and manually update them for each build.","Split the large Java application into multiple smaller AWS CodeBuild projects."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the most effective solution. AWS CodeBuild allows configuring a cache, either local to the build environment or backed by an Amazon S3 bucket, to store dependencies and intermediate build artifacts. This significantly reduces build times by avoiding repeated downloads and recompilations. Option A (incorrect) might offer some speed improvement but is often more costly and less efficient than caching, especially for dependency downloads. Option C (incorrect) AWS CodeArtifact is for managing private packages, but manually updating dependencies for each build does not automate the caching process within CodeBuild. Option D (incorrect) splitting the application might be a good architectural practice but doesn''t directly address the problem of repeated dependency downloads for each individual build project.', 36, 'm_36_1', 9, NULL, NULL);

