INSERT INTO public.mock_test_questions (mock_test_id, question_text, options, question_type, correct_answer, explanation, topic_id, module_id, question_order, pairs, matches)
VALUES
  ('mock_36_all_1775004784055', 'A development team is deploying a new version of a containerized application to an Amazon ECS cluster. They want to ensure that the new version is stable and performs as expected before completely replacing the old version. A gradual traffic shift is required, and the ability to automatically revert if performance metrics degrade. Which deployment strategy should they use with AWS CodeDeploy for Amazon ECS?', '["An in-place deployment strategy that updates tasks one by one.","A blue/green deployment strategy with an immediate 100% traffic shift.","A rolling update strategy managed by Amazon ECS service scheduler.","A canary deployment strategy using AWS CodeDeploy with Amazon CloudWatch alarms for automatic rollback."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{3}'::integer[], 'Option D (correct) describes a canary deployment strategy for Amazon ECS using AWS CodeDeploy. This strategy allows for a gradual traffic shift to the new application version, typically in increments (e.g., 10%, 25%, 100%). By integrating with Amazon CloudWatch alarms, CodeDeploy can automatically roll back to the previous stable version if performance metrics (like error rates or latency) degrade during the rollout. Option A (incorrect) an in-place deployment is not a standard or recommended strategy for containerized applications on ECS, which are designed for immutable deployments. Option B (incorrect) a blue/green deployment with an immediate 100% traffic shift does not allow for a gradual traffic shift or testing with a small percentage of users. Option C (incorrect) a rolling update managed by ECS is a basic deployment type but lacks the advanced traffic shifting and automated rollback capabilities of CodeDeploy''s canary strategy.', 36, 'm_36_4', 10, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_36_all_1775004784055', 'A serverless application using AWS Lambda functions needs to be updated. The team wants to introduce the new function version to a small percentage of invocations, monitor its performance, and then gradually shift all traffic to it. If any issues are detected, traffic should automatically revert to the previous stable version. Which AWS service combination provides this controlled rollout and automated rollback for Lambda?', '["Update the Lambda function directly, then manually monitor Amazon CloudWatch logs for errors.","Use AWS Step Functions to manage the deployment workflow and manual traffic shifting.","Implement a Lambda alias with traffic shifting configured via AWS CodeDeploy and monitored by Amazon CloudWatch alarms.","Create a new Lambda function for each version and manually update API Gateway to point to the new version."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is the recommended approach for controlled rollouts and automated rollbacks for AWS Lambda functions. By using Lambda aliases, you can configure traffic shifting to direct a percentage of invocations to a new function version. AWS CodeDeploy integrates with Lambda aliases to manage this traffic shifting, and Amazon CloudWatch alarms can be configured to trigger automatic rollbacks if performance or error rate thresholds are breached. Option A (incorrect) relies on manual monitoring and lacks automated traffic shifting or rollback capabilities. Option B (incorrect) AWS Step Functions can orchestrate workflows but is not designed for direct traffic shifting and automated rollback for Lambda versions; CodeDeploy is the purpose-built service for this. Option D (incorrect) manually updating API Gateway for each version is cumbersome, error-prone, and lacks automated traffic shifting and rollback.', 36, 'm_36_4', 11, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_36_all_1775004784055', 'A DevOps team is deploying a new version of a microservice to an Amazon EKS cluster. They need a deployment strategy that allows them to maintain two distinct environments (old and new versions) simultaneously, test the new version thoroughly, and then switch all traffic to it instantly, with an easy option to revert to the old environment if necessary. Which deployment strategy should they use with AWS CodeDeploy for Amazon EKS?', '["Rolling update deployment, gradually replacing old pods with new ones within the same service.","Blue/green deployment, deploying the new version to a separate set of pods and then instantly shifting traffic.","Canary deployment, slowly shifting a small percentage of traffic to the new version.","In-place deployment, updating the container image tag directly on existing pods."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) describes a blue/green deployment strategy, which is ideal for this scenario. With AWS CodeDeploy for Amazon EKS, a blue/green deployment involves deploying the new version (''green'') to a completely separate set of pods alongside the existing (''blue'') version. This allows for thorough testing of the ''green'' environment. Once validated, traffic can be instantly shifted from ''blue'' to ''green''. If issues arise, traffic can be instantly reverted to the ''blue'' environment, providing zero downtime and easy rollback. Option A (incorrect) a rolling update replaces pods incrementally, which doesn''t allow for maintaining two distinct environments simultaneously for testing and instant cutover. Option C (incorrect) a canary deployment shifts traffic gradually, which doesn''t meet the ''instantly shifting traffic'' requirement. Option D (incorrect) an in-place deployment is generally not recommended for EKS as it can lead to service disruption and is less robust for rollbacks.', 36, 'm_36_4', 12, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_36_all_1775004784055', 'A company needs to update a legacy application running on a single Amazon EC2 instance. Due to the application''s architecture, it cannot easily support blue/green deployments. The requirement is to update the application with minimal manual effort and ensure the instance is always running the latest approved version. Which AWS CodeDeploy strategy provides the LEAST operational overhead for this scenario?', '["Manually connect to the EC2 instance via SSH and run update scripts.","Use AWS Systems Manager State Manager to apply updates periodically.","Implement an in-place deployment with AWS CodeDeploy, configuring an AppSpec file and lifecycle hooks.","Rebuild the EC2 instance from a new AMI for every application update."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is the most suitable and lowest-overhead solution for this specific scenario. An in-place deployment with AWS CodeDeploy automates the process of updating an application on existing EC2 instances. By defining an AppSpec file and lifecycle hooks, CodeDeploy can manage the entire update process, including stopping the application, installing the new version, and restarting it, all with minimal manual effort. Option A (incorrect) involves significant manual effort, is error-prone, and does not scale. Option B (incorrect) AWS Systems Manager State Manager can manage configurations but CodeDeploy is purpose-built for application deployments with features like lifecycle hooks and rollback. Option D (incorrect) rebuilding the EC2 instance from a new AMI for every update is an immutable infrastructure pattern, which is generally good, but for a single legacy instance that ''cannot easily support blue/green'', it might introduce more complexity and overhead (e.g., AMI management, instance provisioning time) than a simple in-place CodeDeploy update.', 36, 'm_36_4', 13, NULL, NULL);

