INSERT INTO public.mock_test_questions (mock_test_id, question_text, options, question_type, correct_answer, explanation, topic_id, module_id, question_order, pairs, matches)
VALUES
  ('mock_36_all_1775004784055', 'A company operates a microservices architecture on Amazon EC2 instances and AWS Fargate containers across several AWS accounts. They need to centralize all application and infrastructure logs for long-term archival and compliance, ensuring logs are easily queryable later. The solution must minimize operational overhead and scale automatically with fluctuating log volumes.', '["Configure AWS CloudTrail Lake to ingest logs from all sources for long-term storage and querying.","Send all logs directly to Amazon CloudWatch Logs from each source, then use CloudWatch Logs Insights for querying.","Utilize Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to stream logs from all sources to Amazon S3, leveraging S3 lifecycle policies for cost-effective long-term archival.","Deploy a self-managed ELK stack on Amazon EC2 instances to collect, store, and query logs from all accounts."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) provides a highly scalable and managed solution for centralized log aggregation and long-term archival. Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose automatically scales to handle fluctuating log volumes and delivers logs to Amazon S3, which is a cost-effective storage solution. S3 lifecycle policies can then manage the transition of logs to lower-cost storage classes like S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval for compliance, minimizing operational overhead. Option A (incorrect) is primarily for auditing AWS API calls, not general application and infrastructure logs. While CloudTrail Lake can store CloudTrail logs, it''s not the primary service for aggregating all types of application and infrastructure logs from EC2 and Fargate. Option B (incorrect) represents an anti-pattern (ap-4.1-001) as sending all logs directly to CloudWatch Logs can lead to per-account silos, making cross-account analysis and long-term archival less efficient and potentially more expensive than S3 for cold data. CloudWatch Logs is better for real-time monitoring and ad-hoc analysis, not primary long-term archival at scale. Option D (incorrect) involves deploying a self-managed ELK stack, which introduces significant operational overhead for setup, maintenance, scaling, and patching, directly violating the requirement to minimize operational overhead.', 39, 'm_39_1', 1, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_36_all_1775004784055', 'A DevOps team manages a critical e-commerce application running on Amazon EC2 instances. They need to collect, aggregate, and analyze application logs in real-time to quickly identify and troubleshoot performance issues and errors. The solution must provide powerful search and visualization capabilities with minimal setup and maintenance.', '["Configure Amazon CloudWatch Logs metric filters and dashboards for key error patterns, using Logs Insights for ad-hoc queries.","Stream logs from EC2 instances to Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose, which then delivers them to Amazon OpenSearch Service for real-time search and visualization.","Send logs to Amazon S3 for storage and use Amazon Athena to query them for troubleshooting.","Implement AWS X-Ray to trace application requests and identify performance bottlenecks across services."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the most suitable solution for real-time log analysis with powerful search and visualization capabilities and minimal setup. Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose provides a fully managed service for streaming logs, and Amazon OpenSearch Service offers a scalable, managed service for log analytics, full-text search, and interactive dashboards (Kibana). This combination minimizes operational overhead compared to self-managed solutions. Option A (incorrect) is good for specific metric-based alerting and ad-hoc log querying, but CloudWatch Logs Insights does not provide the persistent, interactive dashboarding and visualization capabilities that OpenSearch Service offers for comprehensive log analysis. Option C (incorrect) uses Amazon S3 and Amazon Athena, which is cost-effective for querying large datasets, but it''s not designed for real-time analysis and visualization required for quick troubleshooting. There would be latency in data availability for analysis. Option D (incorrect) is excellent for distributed tracing and visualizing service maps to identify performance bottlenecks, but it is not designed for general application log collection, aggregation, and real-time search/visualization of log content.', 39, 'm_39_1', 2, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_36_all_1775004784055', 'A development team needs to collect custom application metrics (e.g., number of successful API calls, processing time per request) from their Amazon EC2 instances. These metrics are crucial for monitoring application health and performance. The solution should be easy to implement within the application code and integrate seamlessly with existing AWS monitoring tools.', '["Use AWS X-Ray to capture custom metrics and visualize them in service maps.","Publish custom application metrics directly to Amazon CloudWatch using the PutMetricData API.","Stream application logs to Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose and then to Amazon S3, creating metrics from log data.","Deploy a custom metrics server on an Amazon EC2 instance to aggregate and store metrics."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the most straightforward and integrated way to collect custom application metrics. Amazon CloudWatch provides a dedicated API (PutMetricData) for publishing custom metrics, which can then be visualized on CloudWatch Dashboards and used for CloudWatch Alarms. This integrates seamlessly with existing AWS monitoring tools and is easy to implement within application code. Option A (incorrect) is designed for distributed tracing and visualizing the flow of requests through microservices, not for collecting general custom application metrics. While X-Ray provides some performance data, it''s not the primary service for custom metric collection. Option C (incorrect) involves streaming logs and then extracting metrics, which is a more complex and indirect approach for collecting specific custom metrics that are already available within the application. Option D (incorrect) introduces significant operational overhead for managing and maintaining a custom metrics server, violating the ease of implementation and integration requirement.', 39, 'm_39_1', 3, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_36_all_1775004784055', 'A company needs to store application logs for 7 years to meet regulatory compliance requirements. Most logs are accessed infrequently after 90 days, but must remain available for audit. The solution should be highly cost-effective for long-term storage while ensuring data integrity.', '["Store all logs in Amazon CloudWatch Logs with a 7-year retention policy.","Stream logs to Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose, which delivers them to Amazon S3, and configure S3 Lifecycle policies to transition data to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 90 days.","Use Amazon OpenSearch Service to store and manage logs for 7 years, leveraging its cold storage tiers.","Archive logs directly to Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive from application servers."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) offers the most cost-effective and scalable solution for long-term log retention with infrequent access. Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose provides a managed way to stream logs to Amazon S3. S3 is highly durable, and its lifecycle policies allow automatic transition of data to lower-cost storage classes like S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval (or S3 Glacier Deep Archive for even longer-term, less frequent access) after a specified period, significantly reducing storage costs while maintaining data availability for audits. Option A (incorrect) would be significantly more expensive for 7 years of storage, especially for infrequently accessed data, compared to S3 Glacier storage classes. Option C (incorrect) is designed for real-time analytics and visualization, and while OpenSearch Service has cold storage tiers, it is generally much more expensive for pure long-term archival than S3 Glacier, making it less cost-effective for this specific requirement. Option D (incorrect) would require custom logic on application servers to directly archive to S3 Glacier Deep Archive, increasing operational overhead and complexity compared to a managed streaming service like Kinesis Data Firehose and S3 lifecycle policies.', 39, 'm_39_1', 4, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_36_all_1775004784055', 'A DevOps team needs to collect logs from various sources (Amazon EC2, AWS Lambda) and route them to multiple destinations: a real-time analytics platform and a long-term archival solution. The routing mechanism must be scalable, reliable, and managed with minimal custom code.', '["Configure CloudWatch Logs subscription filters to send logs to two separate AWS Lambda functions, each forwarding to a different destination.","Use Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to ingest logs and configure it with multiple delivery streams, one for Amazon OpenSearch Service and another for Amazon S3.","Send logs directly from each source to both Amazon OpenSearch Service and Amazon S3 using custom log agents.","Implement AWS X-Ray to collect logs and traces, then export them to both destinations."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) provides a scalable, reliable, and managed solution with minimal custom code for routing logs to multiple destinations. Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose can ingest logs from various sources and deliver them to multiple destinations, including Amazon OpenSearch Service for real-time analytics and Amazon S3 for long-term archival. This is a fully managed service, reducing operational overhead. Option A (incorrect) involves creating and managing custom AWS Lambda functions, which increases operational overhead and requires writing and maintaining custom code, violating the ''minimal custom code'' constraint. Option C (incorrect) requires deploying and managing custom log agents on each source, which adds significant operational complexity and overhead, especially for multiple destinations. Option D (incorrect) is designed for distributed tracing and not for general log collection and routing to multiple destinations. X-Ray data is distinct from application logs.', 39, 'm_39_1', 5, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_36_all_1775004784055', 'A company runs containerized applications on Amazon ECS and AWS Fargate. They need to aggregate container logs from all tasks into a centralized repository for monitoring and troubleshooting. The solution must be integrated with the container orchestration service and require minimal configuration per task definition.', '["Configure the container log driver to send logs directly to Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose.","Use Amazon CloudWatch Logs as the log driver for container tasks, then configure CloudWatch Logs subscription filters to stream logs to Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose for further processing.","Mount Amazon EFS volumes to each container task for log storage, then use a sidecar container to ship logs to Amazon S3.","Implement AWS X-Ray agents within each container to collect and aggregate logs centrally."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) provides an integrated and efficient solution with minimal configuration. Amazon ECS and AWS Fargate natively support Amazon CloudWatch Logs as a log driver, making it very easy to configure in task definitions. Once logs are in CloudWatch Logs, subscription filters can reliably stream them to Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose for further processing, such as delivery to Amazon S3 or Amazon OpenSearch Service. Option A (incorrect) requires a more complex custom log driver configuration to send directly to Kinesis Data Firehose, which is not as natively integrated or straightforward as CloudWatch Logs for ECS/Fargate. Option C (incorrect) involves managing EFS volumes and sidecar containers, adding significant operational complexity and overhead, and is not the most efficient way to centralize logs. Option D (incorrect) is for distributed tracing and performance monitoring of application requests, not for collecting and aggregating general container logs.', 39, 'm_39_1', 6, NULL, NULL);

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_39_2
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.mock_test_questions (mock_test_id, question_text, options, question_type, correct_answer, explanation, topic_id, module_id, question_order, pairs, matches)
VALUES
  ('mock_36_all_1775004784055', 'A DevOps team wants to proactively detect unusual behavior in their application''s performance metrics (e.g., CPU utilization, request latency) without setting static thresholds that often lead to false positives or missed incidents. The solution should automatically learn normal patterns and alert on significant deviations.', '["Configure Amazon CloudWatch Alarms with static thresholds for each metric.","Implement Amazon CloudWatch Anomaly Detection on relevant metrics and create alarms based on the anomaly detection band.","Stream all metrics to Amazon OpenSearch Service and use its machine learning capabilities for anomaly detection.","Use AWS X-Ray to identify performance issues and manually set alarms based on observed trace data."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) directly addresses the requirement for proactive detection of unusual behavior with fewer false positives. Amazon CloudWatch Anomaly Detection uses machine learning to continuously analyze past metric data, create a baseline of expected values, and identify statistically significant deviations. Alarms configured on this anomaly detection band dynamically adjust to normal fluctuations, making them more effective than static thresholds. Option A (incorrect) represents an anti-pattern (ap-4.2-001) because static thresholds often fail to account for normal variations in metrics, leading to frequent false alarms or missed actual incidents, especially in dynamic environments. Option C (incorrect) would involve streaming all metrics to Amazon OpenSearch Service, which adds significant operational overhead and cost compared to CloudWatch''s native anomaly detection capabilities for metrics. Option D (incorrect) is primarily for distributed tracing and understanding service dependencies. While it helps identify performance issues, it does not provide an automated, machine learning-driven anomaly detection system for metrics, and manually setting alarms based on trace data would be inefficient.', 39, 'm_39_2', 7, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_36_all_1775004784055', 'A company has adopted a microservices architecture, making it challenging to trace requests across multiple services, identify performance bottlenecks, and understand service dependencies. They need a solution to visualize the end-to-end flow of requests and pinpoint latency issues within the distributed system.', '["Use Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights to query and analyze logs from all microservices.","Implement AWS X-Ray to trace requests across microservices and visualize the service map.","Aggregate all application metrics into Amazon CloudWatch Dashboards for performance monitoring.","Configure Amazon EventBridge to capture events from each service and log them for analysis."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the purpose-built service for distributed tracing in a microservices architecture. AWS X-Ray allows developers to analyze and debug production, distributed applications, such as those built using microservices. It provides an end-to-end view of requests as they travel through the application, showing a service map, latency details, and errors, which is crucial for identifying performance bottlenecks and understanding service dependencies. Option A (incorrect) is useful for querying and analyzing log data, but it does not provide the visual, end-to-end tracing and service map capabilities that X-Ray offers for distributed systems. Option C (incorrect) is for monitoring aggregated metrics and creating dashboards, which provides a high-level view of performance but lacks the detailed request-level tracing needed to pinpoint issues within a distributed system. Option D (incorrect) is for event-driven architectures and routing events, not for tracing the execution flow of a single request across multiple services.', 39, 'm_39_2', 8, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_36_all_1775004784055', 'An operations team needs to create custom dashboards to monitor key performance indicators (KPIs) and operational metrics across their applications and infrastructure. They require a solution that can combine metrics from various AWS services and custom application metrics into a single, customizable view.', '["Utilize Amazon CloudWatch Dashboards to visualize metrics from various AWS services and custom application metrics.","Export all metrics to Amazon S3 and use Amazon QuickSight to create business intelligence dashboards.","Set up Amazon OpenSearch Service Dashboards to visualize metrics ingested from CloudWatch.","Implement AWS X-Ray to generate service maps and performance insights for application metrics."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) is the native and most integrated solution for creating custom dashboards with metrics from various AWS services and custom application metrics. Amazon CloudWatch Dashboards allow users to create customizable home pages in the CloudWatch console to monitor resources in a single view, combining metrics, logs, and alarms. This directly meets the requirement for a single, customizable view. Option B (incorrect) involves exporting metrics to S3 and using QuickSight, which is a powerful BI tool but introduces additional complexity and latency for operational monitoring compared to CloudWatch''s real-time dashboards. QuickSight is typically used for business intelligence, not real-time operational monitoring. Option C (incorrect) would require ingesting CloudWatch metrics into OpenSearch Service, which adds significant operational overhead and cost. OpenSearch Service Dashboards are primarily for visualizing log data, not the native CloudWatch metrics. Option D (incorrect) is for distributed tracing and service maps, providing insights into application request flows, not for general-purpose dashboarding of KPIs and operational metrics across infrastructure.', 39, 'm_39_2', 9, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_36_all_1775004784055', 'When a critical application metric (e.g., error rate) deviates from its normal baseline, the DevOps team needs to quickly identify which specific dimension (e.g., EC2 instance ID, API endpoint, container name) is contributing most to the anomaly. They need a tool that can automatically analyze and surface these "top contributors."', '["Use Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights to manually query and filter logs to find contributing dimensions.","Implement Amazon CloudWatch Contributor Insights to analyze log data and identify top contributors to metric anomalies.","Configure AWS X-Ray to trace requests and identify the specific service causing the anomaly.","Stream all logs to Amazon OpenSearch Service and build custom aggregations to find top contributors."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is purpose-built for this exact scenario. Amazon CloudWatch Contributor Insights analyzes log data to provide a real-time view of the top-N contributors to any log-based metric, such as error rates or latency. It automatically identifies which specific dimensions (e.g., EC2 instance ID, API endpoint) are contributing most to a metric''s behavior, especially during anomalies, allowing for quick root cause analysis. Option A (incorrect) requires manual querying and filtering, which is time-consuming and reactive, not providing the automated ''top contributor'' analysis. Option C (incorrect) is for distributed tracing and visualizing service dependencies. While it can help identify a problematic service, it doesn''t automatically pinpoint the top contributing dimensions within log data that Contributor Insights provides. Option D (incorrect) can achieve similar results but requires significant setup, configuration, and management of an OpenSearch Service cluster and custom aggregations, which is more complex and has higher operational overhead than the managed Contributor Insights service.', 39, 'm_39_2', 10, NULL, NULL);

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_39_3
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.mock_test_questions (mock_test_id, question_text, options, question_type, correct_answer, explanation, topic_id, module_id, question_order, pairs, matches)
VALUES
  ('mock_36_all_1775004784055', 'A DevOps team wants to automate remediation actions when specific Amazon CloudWatch alarms trigger (e.g., an EC2 instance CPU utilization alarm). The automation should be event-driven and allow for flexible targeting of various AWS services or custom scripts.', '["Configure Amazon CloudWatch alarms to directly invoke an AWS Lambda function that performs the remediation.","Manually monitor CloudWatch dashboards and execute remediation scripts when alarms are in ALARM state.","Configure Amazon CloudWatch alarms to send state changes to Amazon EventBridge, which then triggers an automated action.","Use Amazon OpenSearch Service to analyze CloudWatch metrics and trigger remediation actions."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is the most flexible and scalable approach for automating remediation actions based on CloudWatch alarms. Amazon CloudWatch alarms can publish state changes as events to Amazon EventBridge. EventBridge then allows you to define rules to match these alarm state change events and route them to various targets, including AWS Lambda functions, AWS Systems Manager Automation documents, Amazon SNS topics, or even other EventBridge event buses. This provides a centralized and flexible way to manage event-driven automation. Option A (incorrect) is a valid approach, but EventBridge offers more flexibility in terms of targets and rule-based routing, especially when multiple types of remediation or different targets are needed for various alarms. Directly invoking Lambda is less flexible if the target needs to change or if more complex routing logic is required. Option B (incorrect) involves manual intervention, which violates the requirement for automation. Option D (incorrect) is for log and metric analysis and visualization, not for directly triggering automated remediation actions based on CloudWatch alarm states.', 39, 'm_39_3', 11, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_36_all_1775004784055', 'A security team needs to automatically respond to security-related events in real-time, such as unauthorized API calls detected by AWS CloudTrail or suspicious activity identified by AWS Security Hub. The response should be immediate and trigger a predefined workflow.', '["Periodically scan AWS CloudTrail logs stored in Amazon S3 using an AWS Lambda function to detect security events.","Create Amazon EventBridge rules that filter AWS CloudTrail events for specific security-related API calls and trigger an automated response.","Use Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights to analyze security logs and manually initiate incident response workflows.","Implement AWS X-Ray to trace suspicious API calls and identify their origin for manual investigation."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the most effective solution for immediate, automated responses to security events. Amazon EventBridge can ingest events directly from AWS CloudTrail (for API calls) and other security services like AWS Security Hub. By creating rules, the security team can filter for specific security-related events and trigger automated actions in real-time, such as invoking an AWS Lambda function to isolate a resource, sending notifications, or initiating a remediation workflow. Option A (incorrect) involves polling S3, which introduces latency and is not a real-time solution for immediate responses. Option C (incorrect) is for interactive log analysis, not for automated, real-time event-driven responses. It requires manual intervention to initiate workflows. Option D (incorrect) is for distributed tracing of application requests, not for detecting and automatically responding to security-related API calls or suspicious activity from CloudTrail or Security Hub.', 39, 'm_39_3', 12, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_36_all_1775004784055', 'An application generates custom events that need to be processed by different downstream services based on event content. The architecture requires a centralized, scalable, and resilient event bus to route these events without tightly coupling the producers and consumers.', '["Use Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to ingest custom events and deliver them to various destinations.","Implement Amazon EventBridge as a central event bus, defining rules to filter and route custom events to appropriate targets based on their content.","Store custom events in Amazon CloudWatch Logs and use CloudWatch Logs Insights to analyze and manually trigger downstream processes.","Deploy a custom message broker on Amazon EC2 instances to handle event routing."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the ideal solution for centralized, scalable, and resilient event routing based on content. Amazon EventBridge acts as a serverless event bus that allows you to define rules to filter events based on their content and route them to multiple targets. This decouples event producers from consumers, enhancing scalability and resilience, and supports custom events. Option A (incorrect) is primarily for streaming data delivery to specific destinations (like S3, OpenSearch, Redshift) and does not offer the same content-based routing capabilities as EventBridge for arbitrary event patterns. Option C (incorrect) is for log storage and analysis, not for real-time event routing and processing by downstream services. It would require manual intervention or complex automation to trigger processes. Option D (incorrect) introduces significant operational overhead for managing and maintaining a custom message broker on EC2 instances, violating the ''minimal custom code'' and ''managed'' aspects of the requirement.', 39, 'm_39_3', 13, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_36_all_1775004784055', 'A DevOps team needs to monitor the health of a custom application running on Amazon EC2 instances by collecting specific application-level metrics (e.g., active user sessions, database connection pool size). They need to visualize these metrics on a dashboard and set alarms if they cross critical thresholds.', '["Publish custom application metrics to Amazon CloudWatch, create CloudWatch Dashboards for visualization, and configure CloudWatch Alarms for threshold breaches.","Implement AWS X-Ray to collect performance data and visualize it in service maps, setting alarms on trace anomalies.","Stream application logs to Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose, then to Amazon OpenSearch Service for metric extraction and dashboarding.","Use AWS CloudTrail Lake to collect application events and create custom SQL queries for health monitoring."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) provides the most integrated and efficient solution for monitoring application health with custom metrics. Amazon CloudWatch allows publishing custom metrics using the PutMetricData API. These metrics can then be easily visualized on CloudWatch Dashboards and used to configure CloudWatch Alarms that trigger when thresholds are breached. This is a native AWS solution designed specifically for monitoring. Option B (incorrect) is for distributed tracing and understanding request flows, not for collecting and alarming on general application-level custom metrics like active user sessions or connection pool size. Option C (incorrect) involves a more complex pipeline for metric collection and dashboarding. While OpenSearch Service can handle metrics, CloudWatch is purpose-built for monitoring and alarming on metrics, offering lower operational overhead for this specific use case. Option D (incorrect) is for auditing AWS API calls, not for collecting application-level custom metrics or monitoring application health.', 39, 'm_39_3', 14, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_36_all_1775004784055', 'An operations team wants to implement an automated workflow that is triggered when a critical CloudWatch alarm enters an ALARM state. This workflow should perform a series of steps, such as notifying on-call personnel, gathering diagnostic information, and attempting a self-healing action.', '["Configure the CloudWatch alarm to send state changes to Amazon EventBridge, which then triggers a predefined workflow.","Manually configure the CloudWatch alarm to send notifications to an Amazon SNS topic, and rely on human intervention for subsequent steps.","Set up Amazon OpenSearch Service to monitor CloudWatch alarms and trigger a custom script for workflow execution.","Use AWS CloudTrail Lake to audit alarm state changes and initiate a workflow based on historical data."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) is the most robust and flexible solution for automating multi-step incident response workflows. Amazon CloudWatch alarms can publish state changes to Amazon EventBridge. EventBridge can then act as the central orchestrator, triggering a predefined workflow (e.g., an AWS Systems Manager Automation document, an AWS Lambda function, or a Step Functions state machine) that performs multiple steps like notifications, diagnostic data collection, and self-healing actions. Option B (incorrect) only handles the notification step and relies on manual intervention for the rest of the workflow, violating the automation requirement. Option C (incorrect) is for log and metric analysis, not for directly orchestrating automated workflows based on CloudWatch alarm states. It would introduce unnecessary complexity. Option D (incorrect) is for auditing API calls and events, not for real-time, automated incident response workflows triggered by CloudWatch alarm state changes. It operates on historical data.', 39, 'm_39_3', 15, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_36_all_1775004784055', 'A company uses several Software-as-a-Service (SaaS) applications and needs to integrate events from these applications into their AWS environment to trigger custom workflows. The solution must provide a secure and scalable way to ingest events from various SaaS providers.', '["Utilize Amazon EventBridge to establish partner event buses, allowing SaaS applications to securely send events to the AWS environment for processing.","Configure Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to ingest events directly from SaaS applications.","Develop custom API Gateway endpoints for each SaaS application to send events to an AWS Lambda function.","Store SaaS application logs in Amazon CloudWatch Logs and create metric filters to detect events."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) is the purpose-built and most secure/scalable solution for ingesting events from SaaS applications. Amazon EventBridge Partner Event Buses allow direct integration with supported SaaS providers, enabling them to securely and reliably send events to your AWS account. This provides a managed, serverless, and scalable way to ingest external events without requiring custom API development or polling. Option B (incorrect) is primarily for streaming data delivery from AWS services or custom producers, not for direct, managed ingestion of events from third-party SaaS applications via partner integrations. Option C (incorrect) would require significant development effort to create and manage API Gateway endpoints and Lambda functions for each SaaS integration, increasing operational overhead and complexity compared to EventBridge''s managed partner integrations. Option D (incorrect) is for log storage and analysis, not for real-time event ingestion and triggering custom workflows based on SaaS events.', 39, 'm_39_3', 16, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_36_all_1775004784055', 'A DevOps team needs to monitor application logs for specific error patterns (e.g., "OutOfMemoryError", "Connection Refused") and trigger an alarm whenever these patterns appear. The solution should be efficient and integrated with their existing monitoring setup.', '["Manually review application logs in Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights at regular intervals for error patterns.","Create Amazon CloudWatch Logs metric filters for the specific error patterns and configure CloudWatch Alarms based on these metrics.","Stream all application logs to Amazon OpenSearch Service and configure custom alerts based on log queries.","Implement AWS X-Ray to detect errors in application traces and trigger alarms based on trace anomalies."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the most efficient and integrated solution for monitoring log patterns and triggering alarms. Amazon CloudWatch Logs allows you to create metric filters that can extract numerical values from log events based on specific patterns (like error messages). These extracted metrics can then be used to create standard Amazon CloudWatch Alarms, which seamlessly integrate with existing monitoring dashboards and notification mechanisms. Option A (incorrect) involves manual review, which is not an automated or efficient solution for real-time alerting. Option C (incorrect) can achieve similar results, but it introduces the operational overhead and cost of managing an OpenSearch Service cluster. For simple log pattern matching and alerting, CloudWatch Logs metric filters and alarms are a more lightweight and integrated solution. Option D (incorrect) is for distributed tracing and identifying errors within the request flow, not for monitoring general application log patterns and triggering alarms based on their occurrence.', 39, 'm_39_3', 17, NULL, NULL);

