INSERT INTO public.mock_test_questions (mock_test_id, question_text, options, question_type, correct_answer, explanation, topic_id, module_id, question_order, pairs, matches)
VALUES
  ('mock_36_all_1775007670439', 'A DevOps team is building a CI/CD pipeline using AWS CodePipeline, AWS CodeBuild, and AWS CodeDeploy across multiple AWS accounts. The security team has mandated that all pipeline actions must adhere strictly to the principle of least privilege. Which IAM strategy should the DevOps team implement to meet this security requirement MOST effectively?', '["Create a single IAM role with administrator access and assign it to the entire AWS CodePipeline to simplify permission management.","Configure separate IAM roles for each stage and action within AWS CodePipeline, granting only the specific permissions required for that particular task.","Use an existing IAM user with programmatic access keys for all CodePipeline actions and store the keys in AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store.","Attach a broad IAM policy directly to the AWS CodePipeline service role, allowing it to perform any action across all AWS services."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the most effective IAM strategy for adhering to the principle of least privilege. By creating separate IAM roles for each stage and action within AWS CodePipeline, and granting only the precise permissions necessary for that specific task (e.g., CodeBuild''s role only needs permissions to pull source, build, and push artifacts), the blast radius of a compromised role is significantly reduced. This directly addresses the anti-pattern [ap-1.1-001] by avoiding overly permissive roles.
Option A (incorrect) represents the anti-pattern [ap-1.1-001] of using a single pipeline role with admin access. This creates a large blast radius if the role is compromised, as it would have permissions to perform actions across many services, violating the principle of least privilege.
Option C (incorrect) is a poor security practice. Using an IAM user''s programmatic access keys directly in a pipeline is less secure than using IAM roles, as keys can be accidentally exposed or hardcoded. IAM roles provide temporary credentials and are the recommended approach for AWS services interacting with each other.
Option D (incorrect) is similar to Option A, granting broad permissions to the CodePipeline service role. This violates the principle of least privilege and creates a significant security risk, as the pipeline would have excessive permissions beyond what is needed for its specific operations.', 36, 'm_36_1', 8, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_36_all_1775007670439', 'A development team frequently builds a large application using AWS CodeBuild. They observe that build times are consistently long due to repeated downloading of external dependencies (e.g., Maven artifacts, npm packages) in every build. The team needs to optimize build performance by reducing the time spent on dependency resolution. Which CodeBuild feature should they implement to achieve this MOST effectively?', '["Increase the compute capacity of the CodeBuild project to use larger build environments.","Manually pre-download all dependencies to an Amazon S3 bucket and then copy them into the CodeBuild environment at the start of each build.","Configure CodeBuild caching to use an Amazon S3 bucket for storing and reusing build dependencies and artifacts across builds.","Refactor the application to eliminate all external dependencies, making each build self-contained."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is the most effective solution for optimizing build performance by reducing dependency download times. AWS CodeBuild''s caching feature, specifically with an Amazon S3 bucket, allows CodeBuild to store and reuse dependencies and intermediate build artifacts across subsequent builds. This significantly reduces the time spent downloading dependencies, as they are retrieved from the cache instead of external sources, directly addressing the performance bottleneck.
Option A (incorrect) might reduce overall build time for compilation or testing phases, but it does not directly address the bottleneck of repeated dependency downloads. While it can speed up other parts of the build, it''s not the most effective solution for dependency resolution specifically.
Option B (incorrect) involves manual pre-downloading and copying, which adds complexity and operational overhead. It requires custom scripting and management, making it less efficient and more error-prone than CodeBuild''s native caching mechanism.
Option D (incorrect) is an impractical and often impossible solution for most modern applications. Eliminating all external dependencies is rarely feasible and would likely require a complete architectural overhaul, which is not a practical solution for optimizing build times within an existing pipeline.', 36, 'm_36_1', 9, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_36_all_1775007670439', 'A global company needs to deploy its application to multiple AWS accounts across different AWS Regions using a single, centralized CI/CD pipeline. The pipeline uses AWS CodePipeline, AWS CodeBuild, and AWS CodeDeploy. The primary requirement is to ensure secure and automated deployments to these target accounts and regions. Which architectural pattern should the DevOps team implement?', '["Create separate, independent CodePipeline instances in each target account and region, and trigger them manually from the central account.","Configure a single CodePipeline in the central account to deploy to target accounts and regions by assuming cross-account IAM roles and using S3 artifact buckets with Cross-Region Replication.","Grant the central CodePipeline''s service role administrator access to all target accounts and regions to simplify cross-account deployments.","Use AWS CodeCommit as the central repository for all deployment scripts and manually execute these scripts from an Amazon EC2 instance in each target account."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) describes the recommended architectural pattern for cross-account and cross-region deployments with AWS CodePipeline. By configuring the central CodePipeline to assume specific, least-privilege IAM roles in the target accounts, it can securely perform actions like deploying with CodeDeploy. Using S3 artifact buckets with Cross-Region Replication ensures that artifacts are available in the target regions, enabling efficient and secure multi-region deployments while adhering to security best practices.
Option A (incorrect) would create a fragmented and difficult-to-manage CI/CD system. Maintaining separate CodePipelines in each target account and region increases operational overhead and makes centralized visibility and control challenging. Manual triggers also defeat the purpose of automation.
Option C (incorrect) violates the principle of least privilege and represents the anti-pattern [ap-1.1-001]. Granting administrator access to the central pipeline''s service role across all target accounts and regions creates a significant security risk, as a compromise of this single role would have broad impact.
Option D (incorrect) involves manual execution of deployment scripts from EC2 instances, which is not an automated CI/CD approach. This method is slow, error-prone, lacks auditability within a pipeline, and significantly increases operational overhead compared to a fully automated CodePipeline.', 36, 'm_36_1', 10, NULL, NULL);

