INSERT INTO public.mock_test_questions (mock_test_id, question_text, options, question_type, correct_answer, explanation, topic_id, module_id, question_order, pairs, matches)
VALUES
  ('mock_36_all_1775007670439', 'A security team needs to automatically detect and remediate Amazon S3 buckets that become publicly accessible, ensuring compliance with internal policies. The solution must provide a centralized dashboard for tracking these non-compliant resources and their remediation status with the LEAST operational overhead. How should a DevOps Engineer implement this solution?', '["Configure AWS Config rules to detect public S3 buckets and enable auto-remediation with AWS Systems Manager Automation documents, then integrate AWS Systems Manager OpsCenter for tracking.","Develop custom AWS Lambda functions to periodically check S3 bucket policies and permissions, triggering Amazon SNS notifications for non-compliance, and manually update a tracking spreadsheet.","Set up Amazon EventBridge rules to capture S3 PutBucketPolicy events, filter for public access, and invoke a custom AWS Lambda function to revert the policy and send an email via Amazon SNS.","Utilize Amazon GuardDuty to detect unusual S3 access patterns, then manually review findings and execute pre-defined AWS Systems Manager Automation runbooks to fix identified issues."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) is the most efficient and integrated approach. AWS Config rules can continuously monitor S3 bucket compliance against predefined or custom rules. Auto-remediation with AWS Systems Manager Automation documents allows for automatic fixing of non-compliant resources, such as reverting public S3 bucket policies. AWS Systems Manager OpsCenter provides a centralized dashboard to track operational issues, including those generated by Config, offering a consolidated view with minimal operational overhead. Option B (incorrect) involves building custom Lambda functions for detection and manual tracking, which introduces significant operational overhead and violates the anti-pattern of manually responding to every AWS Config non-compliance finding by requiring custom logic for detection and manual tracking. Option C (incorrect) relies on real-time event capture but requires custom Lambda logic for remediation and does not inherently offer a centralized tracking dashboard like OpsCenter, increasing custom development and operational burden. Option D (incorrect) uses Amazon GuardDuty for detection, which is focused on threat detection rather than continuous compliance enforcement, and requires manual review and execution of runbooks, which does not meet the ''LEAST operational overhead'' constraint for automated remediation.', 40, 'm_40_2', 1, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_36_all_1775007670439', 'A development team wants to ensure that all newly created Amazon EC2 instances adhere to specific security baselines, such as having a particular Amazon EBS encryption configuration. If an instance is launched without the correct encryption, it should be automatically terminated, and an incident should be logged for review. Which approach meets these requirements with the MOST automation?', '["Implement an AWS Config rule to detect non-compliant EC2 instances regarding EBS encryption, and configure an auto-remediation action to invoke an AWS Systems Manager Automation document that terminates the instance and creates an OpsItem in AWS Systems Manager OpsCenter.","Create an Amazon EventBridge rule to capture EC2 RunInstances API calls, filter for specific tags, and trigger an AWS Lambda function to check EBS encryption, then manually terminate non-compliant instances and log incidents in a ticketing system.","Develop a custom script that runs on a cron job on an Amazon EC2 instance to list all running EC2 instances, check their EBS encryption status, and use the AWS CLI to terminate non-compliant ones, sending a notification via Amazon SNS.","Use AWS CloudTrail to monitor EC2 RunInstances events, set up Amazon SNS topic subscriptions for notifications, and have a security team member manually verify and terminate non-compliant instances, logging details in AWS Systems Manager Incident Manager."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) provides the most automated and integrated solution. AWS Config rules continuously evaluate resource compliance, and when a non-compliant EC2 instance is detected (e.g., incorrect EBS encryption), an auto-remediation action can be triggered. This action can invoke an AWS Systems Manager Automation document to perform the termination. Integrating with AWS Systems Manager OpsCenter automatically creates an OpsItem for tracking, ensuring visibility and a centralized record of the incident. Option B (incorrect) requires custom Lambda development for detection and manual termination, which is less automated and increases operational overhead compared to AWS Config''s built-in capabilities. Option C (incorrect) relies on a self-managed EC2 instance and custom scripting, which is an anti-pattern for undifferentiated heavy lifting, lacks native integration with AWS compliance and incident management services, and introduces significant operational overhead. Option D (incorrect) involves manual verification and termination by a security team member, which does not meet the ''MOST automation'' requirement and embodies the anti-pattern of manually responding to every non-compliance finding.', 40, 'm_40_2', 2, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_36_all_1775007670439', 'A large enterprise requires a standardized process for managing critical incidents, including defined escalation paths, runbooks, and post-incident analysis. The solution needs to integrate with existing monitoring tools and provide a clear timeline of events and actions taken during an incident. Which AWS service is purpose-built to meet these requirements?', '["AWS Systems Manager Incident Manager","AWS Systems Manager OpsCenter","Amazon EventBridge","Amazon SNS"]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) is AWS Systems Manager Incident Manager, which is specifically designed for structured incident response. It provides capabilities for defining escalation plans, runbooks, managing incident contacts, and generating post-incident analysis reports. It integrates with various AWS monitoring services and provides a timeline of events and actions. Option B (incorrect) AWS Systems Manager OpsCenter is for centralizing operational issues (OpsItems) and executing runbooks, but it lacks the structured incident response, escalation management, and post-incident analysis features of Incident Manager. Option C (incorrect) Amazon EventBridge is an event bus service for routing events, not for managing the lifecycle of an incident. While it can trigger actions in response to events, it doesn''t provide the comprehensive incident management framework. Option D (incorrect) Amazon SNS is a messaging service used for sending notifications, which is a component of incident response but does not provide the full incident management capabilities required.', 40, 'm_40_2', 3, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_36_all_1775007670439', 'A company needs to implement a custom remediation for a specific type of resource misconfiguration that AWS Config''s managed rules do not cover directly. This remediation should be triggered automatically when the misconfiguration is detected and involve executing a series of specific API calls. Which combination of services should a DevOps Engineer use to achieve this with the LEAST custom routing logic?', '["Configure an AWS Config custom rule to detect the misconfiguration, then use an Amazon EventBridge rule to capture the Config non-compliance event and trigger an AWS Lambda function that executes the remediation API calls.","Write a custom application that continuously polls AWS Config for non-compliant resources, and upon detection, executes the remediation API calls directly from the application.","Set up an Amazon EventBridge rule to capture all AWS API calls, filter for specific resource changes, and then invoke an AWS Lambda function to check for misconfiguration and perform remediation.","Manually review AWS Config non-compliance reports daily, and if a misconfiguration is found, manually execute an AWS Systems Manager Automation document to remediate the issue."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) provides the most automated and integrated solution with minimal custom routing logic. An AWS Config custom rule can be defined to detect the specific misconfiguration. When this rule detects non-compliance, it emits an event to Amazon EventBridge. An EventBridge rule can then capture this specific Config event and directly invoke an AWS Lambda function to perform the custom remediation API calls. This leverages native AWS services for detection, event routing, and execution. Option B (incorrect) involves building a custom polling application, which is an anti-pattern as it adds undifferentiated heavy lifting for custom event routing and detection logic, increasing operational overhead. Option C (incorrect) attempts to capture all API calls via EventBridge, which would be overly broad and inefficient for detecting a specific misconfiguration, requiring complex filtering within Lambda and potentially missing the initial non-compliance event from Config. Option D (incorrect) involves manual review and remediation, which directly violates the requirement for automation and embodies the anti-pattern of manually responding to every AWS Config non-compliance finding.', 40, 'm_40_2', 4, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_36_all_1775007670439', 'A DevOps team needs a centralized place to view, investigate, and resolve operational issues across their AWS accounts. They require the ability to aggregate operational data, execute predefined runbooks for common problems, and integrate with existing monitoring and alerting systems. Which AWS service is best suited for this purpose?', '["AWS Systems Manager OpsCenter","AWS Systems Manager Incident Manager","Amazon EventBridge","Amazon SNS"]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) is AWS Systems Manager OpsCenter. It provides a centralized console where operational issues (OpsItems) are aggregated. From OpsCenter, engineers can view contextual information, execute AWS Systems Manager Automation runbooks to diagnose and remediate problems, and integrate with other monitoring services. Option B (incorrect) AWS Systems Manager Incident Manager is designed for structured incident response, including escalation paths and post-incident analysis, which goes beyond just aggregating and resolving general operational issues. While related, OpsCenter is the primary tool for the daily management of operational issues. Option C (incorrect) Amazon EventBridge is an event bus service for routing events to various targets, not a centralized dashboard for operational issue management. It can be used to trigger actions that create OpsItems but doesn''t provide the management interface itself. Option D (incorrect) Amazon SNS is a messaging service for notifications, which can be part of an alerting system but does not offer the centralized issue tracking and runbook execution capabilities required.', 40, 'm_40_2', 5, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_36_all_1775007670439', 'A company needs to ensure that all Amazon EC2 instances are regularly patched for security vulnerabilities. They want to automate the detection of unpatched instances and trigger a patching process without manual intervention. The solution should also allow for custom patching schedules and target specific instance groups. Which combination of services should be used?', '["Configure AWS Config rules to identify unpatched EC2 instances and use AWS Systems Manager Automation documents, integrated with AWS Systems Manager Patch Manager, to automatically apply patches based on defined schedules and targets.","Develop a custom AWS Lambda function that periodically scans EC2 instances for patch compliance, and if non-compliant, triggers a manual patching process via SSH commands.","Set up Amazon EventBridge rules to capture EC2 state changes, and then invoke an AWS Lambda function to check patch levels and initiate patching using a custom script stored in Amazon S3.","Manually run AWS Systems Manager Patch Manager on a weekly basis, selecting specific instance groups and applying patches after a team review process."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) provides the most automated and integrated solution for patch management. AWS Config rules can continuously monitor EC2 instances for patch compliance. When non-compliance is detected, an AWS Systems Manager Automation document can be triggered. This Automation document can then leverage AWS Systems Manager Patch Manager to apply the necessary patches, adhering to custom schedules and targeting specific instance groups. This approach is highly automated and uses native AWS services. Option B (incorrect) involves custom Lambda development for scanning and manual patching via SSH, which introduces significant operational overhead, is less secure, and is an anti-pattern for undifferentiated heavy lifting. Option C (incorrect) uses EventBridge and Lambda for detection, but relies on a custom script in Amazon S3 for patching, which is less robust and integrated than Patch Manager. Option D (incorrect) involves manual execution of Patch Manager, which does not meet the requirement for automation and embodies the anti-pattern of manually responding to every AWS Config non-compliance finding.', 40, 'm_40_2', 6, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_36_all_1775007670439', 'A DevOps team needs to automatically respond to AWS Health events, specifically when an Amazon EC2 instance is scheduled for retirement. The response should involve creating a new EC2 instance, migrating data, and then terminating the old instance, all without human intervention. Which combination of services provides the MOST automated and robust solution?', '["Create an Amazon EventBridge rule that captures AWS Health events for EC2 instance retirement, and configure it to trigger an AWS Lambda function that orchestrates the instance replacement and data migration using AWS Systems Manager Automation documents.","Periodically poll the AWS Health Dashboard using a custom script running on an Amazon EC2 instance, and upon detecting a retirement event, manually initiate the replacement process.","Set up an Amazon SNS topic to receive AWS Health notifications, and subscribe an email address to alert a human operator, who will then manually perform the instance replacement.","Use AWS Config rules to monitor EC2 instance state changes, and if an instance is in a ''stopping'' state, trigger an AWS Systems Manager Automation document to create a new instance and terminate the old one without considering the specific retirement event."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) is the most automated and robust solution. AWS Health events are published to Amazon EventBridge, allowing for direct capture of specific events like EC2 instance retirement. An EventBridge rule can filter for these events and trigger an AWS Lambda function. The Lambda function can then orchestrate complex workflows, such as creating a new instance, migrating data, and terminating the old one, by invoking AWS Systems Manager Automation documents, which are ideal for executing predefined operational tasks. Option B (incorrect) involves custom polling and manual intervention, which is an anti-pattern for undifferentiated heavy lifting and does not meet the ''MOST automated'' requirement. Option C (incorrect) relies on human intervention for the entire process, failing to meet the automation requirement. Option D (incorrect) uses AWS Config to monitor state changes, which might not specifically target retirement events and could trigger prematurely or incorrectly, potentially leading to unintended actions or missing the specific context of a scheduled retirement.', 40, 'm_40_2', 7, NULL, NULL);

